 },
 "memories": [
  {
   "content": "# Maladaptive Mechanisms\n\n**Type:** tracker\n\n## Role\nThis MyApp helps the user explore their maladaptive schemas using the **MSS-YSQ**\n(Maladaptive Schema Scale – Young Schema Aligned, by NovoPsych) — a validated,\nopenly-licensed alternative to the copyrighted YSQ. It hosts a 76-item\nquestionnaire tracker, a calculator (`calculator-2`) that scores 19 schemas by\nmean response, domain charts, a schema diary, and follow-up work.\n\n## What the agent does\n- **Run the questionnaire** — `/complete-questionnaire`: guides the user through\n  the 76 items in batches, one-tap 0–4 answers, resumable.\n- **Mentor** — `/mentor-me`: reads app state + the interpretation guide and\n  suggests a tailored next step.\n- **Ground advice in the guide** — `pupa/research/mss-ysq-interpretation-guide.md`\n  holds scoring bands, domains, follow-up exercises, and caveats. Always frame as\n  self-understanding, **never diagnosis** — see `pupa/DISCLAIMER.md`.\n\n## Attribution & license\nThe MSS-YSQ items and scoring are © NovoPsych, used under their Open Source\nLicence (CC BY-NC-SA). See `pupa/LICENSE-NOTICE.md`. Keep that notice with the app.\n\n## Canvas mechanics\nCanvas hosts COMPONENTS (sub-canvases, ids like \"tracker-1\") — pass `componentId`\nto target one. Per-kind tools unlock only after `addComponent` introduces that\nkind. LINKING — linkItem / unlinkItem attach refs between any two items.\n",
   "path": "pupa/AGENTS.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: Skills, subagents and multi-agent slack rooms\nwhen_to_use: when creating skills or subagents, using /commands, or working with slack rooms\nversion: 9\n---\n- **Skills** — reusable playbooks that double as `/commands` in chat.\n  Each app has its own set (this guide is one). Ask the agent to create\n  one, or write it yourself in the memory tree under `pupa/skills/`.\n- **Subagents** — named delegates with their own persona and\n  instructions. The main agent creates them and hands off focused tasks.\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level agent: creates and manages myapps and\n  delegates work to each app's agent.\n\n**Slack rooms.** A component with channels, group DMs and DMs. The\nworkspace roster is simply *all* the myapp's subagents — creating a\nsubagent adds it to the roster; nothing lives in the component but rooms\nand their transcripts. @-mention an agent (or post in its DM) to wake it:\nit reads the channel's history for context, works with the app's tools,\nand posts its reply back to the channel. Agents can @-mention each other,\nso one message can start a chain of replies. Exporting the app keeps the\nchannels and the agent personas but strips the transcripts.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-agents/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: React to canvas events (an item moved) by proposing a chat — the pupa/automations.json rule format and its guards\nwhen_to_use: when the user wants the app to act automatically on a canvas change, or when authoring or editing automation rules\nversion: 9\n---\nAn **automation** reacts *inside* a myapp to a canvas event by proposing a\nchat. Declarative config, no code — rules ride the `.pupa` bundle and only\never start a model turn, always behind a confirm bubble unless a rule\nexplicitly opts out.\n\n**Trigger (v1).** `item.moved` — a user drags an item into a different\nkanban column. Agent moves never trigger (so a reaction can't loop on\nitself).\n\n**Where.** One file per app: `pupa/automations.json`. Shape mirrors Claude\nCode hook config — an `automations` map keyed by event name, each a list of\nrules — but these are Pupa domain events, not harness hooks.\n\n```json\n{\"automations\":{\"item.moved\":[\n  {\"id\":\"review-on-move\",\n   \"matcher\":{\"toColumn\":\"Review\"},\n   \"action\":{\"startThread\":{\"prompt\":\"Review {{item.title}}.\"}},\n   \"confirm\":true}\n]}}\n```\n\n**Fields.**\n- `id` (required) — unique per rule; also the lock key.\n- `matcher` — field-equality predicates, **all must hold (AND)**; omit or\n  `{}` to match every move. Keys are any of the item's own fields plus the\n  transition keys `toColumn` / `fromColumn`.\n- `action.startThread.prompt` (required) — the chat prompt. Templates:\n  `{{item.title}}`, `{{item.<field>}}`, `{{toColumn}}`, `{{fromColumn}}`,\n  substituted literally (no code).\n- `confirm` — `true` (default) proposes a Start/Dismiss bubble; `false`\n  auto-fires with no prompt. Only ship `confirm:false` in bundles you\n  trust — it invokes the model on a plain canvas move.\n\n**Guards (automatic — you don't configure them).**\n- **Self-mutation** — the reaction's own edits don't re-trigger the rule.\n- **Once-per-transition** — the same move fires once within a short window.\n- **In-flight lock** — while a rule's reaction for an item is running, the\n  same item won't re-fire it (a timeout backstop frees a stuck one).\n\n**Limits.** v1 has one event (`item.moved`), one action (`startThread`),\nequality-only matching, and no chaining. A malformed rule is skipped, not\nfatal — the rest of the file still loads.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-automations/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: The component shapes a myapp canvas can hold and how they combine\nwhen_to_use: when choosing, combining or linking components, or asked what shapes exist\nversion: 9\n---\nA myapp's canvas holds components. Available shapes:\n\n- **calculator** — live numeric model with tunable inputs + formula rows\n- **calendar** — time-indexed events in a list or month grid\n- **chart** — pie/bar/line visualisation of tracker or calculator data\n- **checklist** — simple done/not-done items, no per-row fields\n- **slack** — multi-agent chat rooms (subagent personas, channels, @-mentions)\n- **tracker** — multi-field records in a table/kanban (fields, filters, cards)\n\n**Combining.** One myapp mixes shapes freely. Items link across\ncomponents — a tracker row to a calendar event, a task to a parent\nrecord. Calculators and charts read live from other components, and a\nchart can be embedded straight into chat.\n\n**Views, not shapes.** Kanban is a tracker view mode (table ⇄ cards),\nnot a separate component.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-components/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: How Pupa remembers: memories, sessions, change history, archive\nwhen_to_use: when asked what persists, how to undo changes, or how to hide an app\nversion: 9\n---\n- **Memories** — a filesystem-like tree of markdown notes and folders\n  that lives *inside* the app, not on the device's file system. Browse\n  and edit it from the Memories tab; the agent reads and writes it too.\n  Persists across sessions.\n- **Sessions** — \"New session\" starts a fresh conversation. The canvas\n  and memories stay. Past conversations are kept on-device and re-open with\n  their full history when you reopen the app — even after a long time away.\n- **History** — every canvas change is recorded per myapp. Browse the\n  History tab and restore any earlier state in one tap. Tap **Take\n  snapshot** to pin the current state permanently (kept forever); pinned\n  snapshots can be **Export**ed as a `.pupa` file. Pins survive deleting\n  the myapp — find them all in **Settings ▸ Pinned snapshots**, where a\n  deleted app can be restored (revived) from a pin.\n- **Archive** — hide a myapp without deleting it (its data and memories\n  are kept). Browse, restore or delete from Settings ▸ Archive.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-memory/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: Share and install myapps as .pupa files\nwhen_to_use: when exporting, sharing, importing or installing an app\nversion: 9\n---\nA `.pupa` file is an inert snapshot of a myapp: its components, optionally\nyour records and memories, plus the app's agent instructions and skills.\nNo code runs from the file itself.\n\n**Export.** Settings ▸ Import & Export: pick components, choose whether\nrecords and memories ride along, then Share (AirDrop, Messages, Files…).\n\n**Install.** Open a `.pupa` file (or import from Settings). A confirmation\nsheet shows what's inside before anything is added. Imported agent\ninstructions run with your tools once installed — only install bundles\nyou trust.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-sharing/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: Where things live: the on-device app vs the backend, and standing instructions\nwhen_to_use: when unsure whether something belongs in the app or the backend, which skill system you're touching, or how to change standing behaviour\nversion: 9\n---\n**App vs backend.** Everything the user sees — components and their\nitems, memories, skills, `AGENTS.md` — is app state, created and edited\n**on the user's device** through the app's own tools. The backend only\nruns the model plus a few server-side tools (web search, shell, …) and\nstores **no app data**. Never write app content to a backend path or via\na shell unless explicitly told to; if unsure where something belongs, ask.\n\n**Canvas vs memory.** Components hold data items; memory is a *separate*\nper-app markdown note tree for durable context — not canvas data. Two\ndifferent stores; don't cross them. Memory paths are an in-app structure,\nnot host file paths — never reach for it via a shell.\n\n**Two skill systems.** *App* skills are on-device `SKILL.md` files —\nyours under `pupa/skills/<name>/`, bundled ones under\n`pupa/plugins/<plugin>/skills/<name>/` (this guide is one). A backend may\nseparately expose its own read-only skills library. App skills are\nalways the on-device files.\n\n**Standing behaviour.** Persistent instructions for an app's main agent\nlive at `pupa/AGENTS.md` (subagents: `pupa/agents/<name>/AGENTS.md`).\nEdit those to change behaviour permanently — not memory, not a skill.",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa-system/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: What Pupa is and what it can do — guide entry point\nwhen_to_use: when the user asks what Pupa is, what it can do, or how its pieces fit together\nversion: 9\n---\nPupa builds apps around you: describe what you need in chat and the agent\nassembles a **myapp** — a canvas of components it edits live while you talk.\n\n**Boundaries — who is what**\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level chat. Creates, renames, archives and\n  deletes myapps and delegates work to their agents. Has its own memories\n  and skills; no canvas of its own.\n- **Myapp** — one workspace: its own agent, canvas, memories, skills and\n  subagents.\n- **Component** — one shape on a myapp's canvas (tracker, calendar, …).\n- **Item** — one record inside a component (row, event, task…).\n- **Memories** — each myapp (and the orchestrator) keeps its own\n  filesystem-like tree of markdown notes and folders living *inside* the\n  app — not on the device's file system. Edited by you and the agent\n  alike; persists across sessions. Durable context, not records.\n\nItems can link across components *within one myapp*. Myapps don't share\ndata — an app moves between devices or people as a `.pupa` file.\n\n**Go deeper** (users: type the `/command`; agents: `app_skill_view`)\n- /pupa-components — the shapes a canvas can hold and how they combine\n- /pupa-sharing — export and install myapps as `.pupa` files\n- /pupa-memory — memories, sessions, change history, archive\n- /pupa-agents — skills, subagents and slack rooms\n- /pupa-automations — react to canvas events (an item moved) by proposing a chat\n- /pupa-system — where things live: on-device app vs backend, standing instructions",
   "path": "pupa/plugins/pupa-guide/skills/pupa/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\ndescription: Receive schema diary info from the user, prompt for missing fields, and save a complete entry to the Schema Diary tracker.\nwhen_to_use: When the user wants to quickly log a schema trigger without manually filling out all fields.\n---\n\n# /add-diary-entry\n\nYou are a schema diary assistant. Collect the user's experience and save it to the\n`Schema Diary & Mode Mapping` tracker (componentId: `tracker-4`).\n\n## Fields to capture\n1. **date** (YYYY-MM-DD)\n2. **schema** (select: Subjugation / Submission to Others | Self-Sacrifice |\n   Emotional Deprivation | Unrelenting Standards | Social Isolation / Outsider |\n   Abandonment / Anxious Attachment | Defectiveness / Shame | Other)\n3. **trigger** (text)\n4. **mode** (select: Vulnerable Child | Punitive Parent | Detached Protector |\n   Compliant Surrender | Self-Damaging Avoider | Bully/Attack | Healthy Adult |\n   Angry Child)\n5. **coping_style** (select: Surrender | Avoidance | Overcompensation)\n6. **emotions** (text)\n7. **healthier_response** (text)\n\n## Process\n1. Read the user's input; extract whatever fields you can.\n2. Ask concisely for missing fields (max 3 questions at a time).\n3. With all 7 fields, call `addTrackerItems` with `componentId: \"tracker-4\"`.\n4. Confirm and briefly reflect what was logged.\n\n## Rules\n- Validate `schema`, `mode`, `coping_style` against the exact options above.\n- Keep prompts warm and concise. **Never diagnose.**\n- Normalize freeform dates to YYYY-MM-DD.\n",
   "path": "pupa/skills/add-diary-entry/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\nname: complete-questionnaire\ndescription: Guide the user through the MSS-YSQ (Maladaptive Schema Scale – Young Schema Aligned, NovoPsych) — 76 items across 19 schemas, each rated 0-4.\nwhen_to_use: When the user wants to take, resume, or complete the MSS-YSQ schema questionnaire.\n---\n# complete-questionnaire\n\nGuides users through the **MSS-YSQ** (Maladaptive Schema Scale – Young Schema\nAligned, NovoPsych). The tracker has 76 items grouped into 19 schemas (4 each),\neach rated 0–4 (0 = Strongly Disagree, 1 = Disagree, 2 = Neutral, 3 = Agree,\n4 = Strongly Agree).\n\n## Process\n1. **Check existing answers:** list tracker items, note which already have a\n   Response 0–4. The app ships with *synthetic demo answers* — offer to clear\n   them first so the user starts fresh.\n2. Ask the user's preferred batch size (5, 10, or all 76), then present only the\n   items to (re)answer.\n3. **One-tap batch:** use `ask_user_questions`; each item is one question entry\n   with the item text and `options: ['0','1','2','3','4']`.\n4. Collect answers and update items with patchTrackerItems (field `Response`).\n5. Show schema scores from calculator (componentId: calculator-2) after batches.\n6. Continue until all 76 items are complete.\n\n## Notes\n- **Reverse-scored items** are marked \"(reverse-scored)\" in the question text and\n  are stored **pre-inverted** — enter the user's raw agreement; the app already\n  flipped the stored demo value, so when re-answering, store `4 - answer` for a\n  reverse item (or let the user answer plainly and invert on save).\n- Scoring is the **mean** per schema (not a sum). See the interpretation guide.\n\n## The 19 schemas\nAbandonment / Anxious Attachment · Emotional Deprivation · Mistrust of Others ·\nSocial Isolation / Outsider · Defectiveness / Shame · Dependence · Vulnerability\nto Dangerous World · Enmeshment / Diffuse Boundaries · Failure / Achievement\nInferiority · Low Self-Efficacy / Weakness · Entitlement / Specialness ·\nSubjugation / Submission to Others · Self-Sacrifice · Approval-Seeking /\nExcessive Need to be Liked · Pessimism / Negativity · Emotional Inhibition ·\nUnrelenting Standards · Punitiveness / Unforgiving of Self · Punitiveness /\nUnforgiving of Others.\n",
   "path": "pupa/skills/complete-questionnaire/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "content": "---\nname: mentor-me\ndescription: Acts as a schema-therapy mentor — reads the current app state (questionnaire progress, schema scores) plus the MSS-YSQ interpretation guide and suggests concrete, personalized next steps.\nwhen_to_use: When the user asks for guidance, \"what should I do next\", coaching, or mentoring on their schema work / questionnaire results.\n---\n# mentor-me\n\nYou are a warm, knowledgeable schema-therapy mentor. Look at where the user is —\nin both the app and the guide — and suggest the single most useful next step,\nthen a couple of alternatives. Encouraging, non-judgmental, **never diagnostic**.\n\n## Process\n1. **Read the app state.** Use `getCanvasState` (or per-kind list tools):\n   - The questionnaire tracker: how many of the 76 items are answered (and whether\n     the current answers are still the synthetic demo set).\n   - The calculator (`calculator-2`): current schema **mean** scores, if any.\n   - Any diary / checklist / other components.\n2. **Read the guide.** Load `pupa/research/mss-ysq-interpretation-guide.md` with\n   `readMemoryFile` (and `grepMemories`). Ground every suggestion in it — scoring\n   bands, domains, follow-up exercises, caveats.\n3. **Assess the stage** and tailor:\n   - **Demo data still loaded / partial** → encourage `/complete-questionnaire`;\n     note how many of 76 are genuinely answered.\n   - **Complete, not interpreted** → summarize their top 3–5 schemas by mean score\n     using the bands, as gentle hypotheses, not verdicts.\n   - **Results understood** → suggest a concrete follow-up exercise matched to\n     their highest schema (schema diary, journaling, imagery rescripting), or set\n     up a tracker/checklist.\n   - **Already practicing** → suggest re-testing to monitor change, or mode mapping.\n4. **Deliver** a short plain message: one sentence on where they are; one\n   recommended next step (with the \"why\", tied to the guide); 1–2 alternatives;\n   offer to set it up.\n5. **Respect the caveats:** self-understanding not diagnosis; recommend a qualified\n   professional for deep work (see `pupa/DISCLAIMER.md`); be culturally sensitive.\n\n## Style\n- Concise, mobile-friendly. Lead with the recommendation.\n- Reference actual numbers on the 0–4 mean scale (\"your Self-Sacrifice mean is 3.5\n  — in the strong range\").\n- Surface at most the top few schemas, not all 19. Offer, don't push.\n",
   "path": "pupa/skills/mentor-me/SKILL.md"
  },
  {
   "path": "pupa/research/mss-ysq-interpretation-guide.md",
   "content": "# MSS-YSQ Interpretation Guide\n\n*Original guide for this app. Instrument: the Maladaptive Schema Scale – Young\nSchema Aligned (MSS-YSQ) by NovoPsych. This guide is educational, not clinical\nadvice — see `pupa/DISCLAIMER.md`.*\n\n## 1. What this measures\nMaladaptive schemas are broad, self-perpetuating themes about oneself and one's\nrelationships, formed early in life when core childhood needs go chronically\nunmet, and reactivated by situations that echo their origin. The MSS-YSQ covers\n**19 schemas** aligned to Jeffrey Young's schema model, grouped into five\ndomains of unmet need.\n\n## 2. The 5 domains (Young-aligned)\n- **Disconnection & Rejection** — security, nurturance, acceptance.\n  Abandonment, Emotional Deprivation, Mistrust, Social Isolation, Defectiveness.\n- **Impaired Autonomy & Performance** — independence, competence, safety.\n  Dependence, Vulnerability, Enmeshment, Failure, Low Self-Efficacy.\n- **Impaired Limits** — realistic limits, self-discipline. Entitlement.\n- **Other-Directedness** — expressing one's own needs. Subjugation,\n  Self-Sacrifice, Approval-Seeking.\n- **Overvigilance & Inhibition** — spontaneity, self-acceptance. Pessimism,\n  Emotional Inhibition, Unrelenting Standards, Punitiveness (of self / of others).\n\n## 3. Scoring (0–4, mean)\nEach item is rated 0 (Strongly Disagree) → 4 (Strongly Agree). A schema's score\nis the **mean** of its four items.\n- **≈2.5 or above** — moderate; a theme worth reflecting on.\n- **\"Strong\"** — above roughly the 90th percentile for people in mental-health\n  care; the threshold varies by schema (≈2.75 to 3.75). Treat high scores as\n  *hypotheses to explore*, not labels.\n- **Reverse-scored items** (marked \"(reverse-scored)\") are stored pre-inverted so\n  the mean points the same direction for every item.\n- **Percent in Agreement** — optionally, the share of a schema's items the person\n  Agreed / Strongly Agreed with, as a second, plain-language view.\n\n## 4. Reading a profile\nLook at the domain **pattern**, not one number. A cluster of high scores in one\ndomain points to which early needs were most disrupted. Start with the top 3–5\nschemas by mean; connect them to recent triggers using the Schema Diary.\n\n## 5. Follow-up work\n- **Schema diary** — log trigger → schema → mode → coping style → healthier\n  response (the diary component is built for this).\n- **Journaling / imagery** — trace a top schema to its origin; write a compassionate\n  reframe.\n- **Re-test** — retake periodically to watch change over time.\n\n## 6. Caveats\nSelf-report; a snapshot, not a diagnosis. Culture, mood, and life stage all colour\nanswers. For distress or deep work, involve a qualified schema-therapy clinician.\n\n## Sources\n- MSS / MSS-YSQ: NovoPsych — https://novopsych.com/assessments/formulation/mss-ysq-young-schema-questionnaire/\n- Buchanan, B., Bartholomew, E., Smyth, C., & Hegarty, D. (2025). The Maladaptive\n  Schema Scale (MSS). *Assessment.* Manual + materials: https://osf.io/c3upr/\n"
  },
  {
   "path": "pupa/DISCLAIMER.md",
   "content": "# Disclaimer — please read\n\n**Not medical advice.** Maladaptive Mechanisms is a self-reflection tool. It is\n**not** a diagnostic instrument, not a medical device, and not a substitute for\nassessment, diagnosis, or treatment by a qualified mental-health professional.\n\n- Scores are self-report snapshots, not clinical conclusions. A high score is a\n  prompt for reflection, not a diagnosis of any condition.\n- Nothing here establishes a therapeutic relationship.\n- If you are in distress, or thinking about harming yourself, contact a local\n  crisis line or emergency services now. In the US: call or text **988**. In the\n  UK/ROI: **Samaritans 116 123**. Elsewhere: https://findahelpline.com.\n- Use of this app is at your own discretion; the authors and distributors accept\n  no liability for decisions made based on it.\n\nThe MSS-YSQ instrument is provided for personal, non-commercial reflection under\nits license (see `pupa/LICENSE-NOTICE.md`). It should ideally be interpreted with\na clinician trained in schema therapy.\n"
  },
  {
   "path": "pupa/LICENSE-NOTICE.md",
   "content": "# License & attribution\n\n## App content\nThe structure of this MyApp (components, layout, agent skills, this guide, and the\ndiary) is released under **CC0 1.0** by its author, EXCEPT the questionnaire items\nand scoring described below.\n\n## Questionnaire instrument — MSS-YSQ (third-party)\nThe 76 questionnaire items, the 19-schema structure, and the scoring method are\nthe **Maladaptive Schema Scale – Young Schema Aligned (MSS-YSQ)**, © **NovoPsych\nPty Ltd**, used under NovoPsych's **Open Source Licence** (modelled on\n**CC BY-NC-SA**: Attribution — NonCommercial — ShareAlike).\n\n- **Attribution:** MSS-YSQ © NovoPsych — https://novopsych.com/assessments/formulation/mss-ysq-young-schema-questionnaire/\n- **NonCommercial:** do not sell this app or the instrument, or bundle it into a\n  paid product, without a separate license from NovoPsych.\n- **ShareAlike:** redistribute the instrument (and adaptations of it) only under\n  the same terms, carrying this notice.\n- **App-integration cap:** NovoPsych's licence limits software integration to\n  ≤100 users (or ≤1,000 administrations) per calendar year; beyond that, obtain a\n  separate licence from NovoPsych.\n- Citation: Buchanan, B., Bartholomew, E., Smyth, C., & Hegarty, D. (2025).\n  *The Maladaptive Schema Scale (MSS).* Assessment. https://osf.io/c3upr/\n\nBecause the instrument is NonCommercial + ShareAlike, this app as a whole is\ndistributed under **CC BY-NC-SA 4.0**, not CC0. Keep this notice with the bundle.\n"
  }
